export const FORCE_SYMMETRY: FleetText[] = [
  { domain: "footprint", text: "Footprint is inheritance before it is policy: 61 cantonments were notified under the 1924 Act and earlier, the ordnance factories date from 1801 (Cossipore) onward, and the DRDO labs cluster where their users sit. Running the one lens this file supports — installations per million residents (177 rows nationally) — on BJP-run large states (UP, MH, MP, GJ, RJ, HR, UT, OR, CT, DL, AS, AP, BR, party as commonly reported in 2026) gives 114 installations / 984m = 0.12 per million; on opposition-run states (TN, KL, KA, WB, PB, TG, JK, HP, JH) 57 / 415.6m = 0.14 per million. The two groups are close and both are dominated by colonial-era cantonments and a few research cities (Bengaluru, Hyderabad, Chennai, Kolkata); the gap in either direction is smaller than the coverage gap (BSF, HAL, BEL, prisons absent). Nothing here supports a story of installations following party. Party is text only." },
  { domain: "literature", text: "Cases: Bofors (Congress era) beside Rafale (BJP era) with identical fields — document trail (Swedish Radio/The Hindu 1987; The Hindu 2019), court record (Delhi HC 2004: no evidence any public servant received a bribe; SC 2018: no reason to intervene, review dismissed 2019), audit (JPC 1988 not opened; CAG 2019 not opened), foreign probe (Swiss documents 1997; French PNF 2021–), outcome (no conviction in either). The lens produces the same shape for both governments: documented process anomalies, documented agent-side or dissent-side paper, and a judiciary that declined to find corruption. AgustaWestland beside Tatra (both UPA-era, both CBI cases from 2012–13): the record is dense for one and a stub for the other, which is a finding about attention, not guilt. Literature: the principal budget analyst (Behera) wrote the IDSA series under both UPA and NDA and the ORF Agnipath brief under NDA; his adequacy measure (projection-vs-allocation gap) was 27% in UPA's last year and 25–30% in 2018–20, so the method does not flatter either side. The SPIR attitude series exists only for 2018–25, so no UPA baseline can be run; this is recorded as a void rather than inferred. States: this domain holds no state rows; the SPIR tables (reserved-post vacancies highest in UP and Haryana; premature transfers highest in Haryana and UP, both BJP-run in 2016; constabulary ratio met only in West Bengal and Bihar, opposition-run) are reported as printed and party is text only." },
  { domain: "money-people", text: "Bonds: the same table was read for every donor and party. Defence-linked donors split 61.8% BJP / 38.2% other parties by value (Megha 60.5% BJP; Mahindra and Cyient 100% BJP), against a BJP share of 46.1% of all matched bonds and 29.1% of donors giving only to the BJP; Megha's other recipients (BRS 195, DMK 85, YSRCP 37, TDP 28, Congress 18, JD(U) 10, JD(S) 5, Janasena 4) show the same denominations and sale windows. The lens (bond amounts by party) produces the same alarm-sized picture for the non-defence donors of every other party (e.g. TMC Rs 1,592 cr, Congress Rs 1,351 cr, BRS Rs 1,191 cr, DMK Rs 632 cr), so a bond alone is not a finding. DPSU control: no matched order lists for HAL/BEL/Mazagon vs private vendors were opened, so only Mahindra Defence's own order is date-tested; Bharat Forge's board page is the board control for retired officers. Cases: Bofors beside Rafale and AgustaWestland beside Tatra are run with identical fields (decision date and government; first allegation; investigator; latest stage; counter-record); UPA-era cases (AgustaWestland, Tatra, Sukna, Adarsh) beside NDA-era cases (Rafale, Pegasus) with the same fields: no final conviction in any of the seven records opened, and every alleged claim ships with the other side's recorded answer." },
  { domain: "pay-pensions", text: "Same lens, both governments, from the demands themselves: service pay ÷ Capital Outlay actual was 0.88 in 2009-10 and 0.78 in 2013-14 (UPA-II, Antony) and 0.90 in 2024-25 (NDA, Rajnath Singh; 0.94 with the Agnipath line), with BE 2026-27 at 0.68 (0.76 with Agnipath): the ratio is flat and the only numerator shocks are the 6th CPC arrears (2009-10) and the Agnipath line (2024-25 →). What moved is pensions — 3.47× from actual 2013-14 to 2024-25 against 2.33× for service pay and 2.02× for capital — driven by the 7th CPC (2016), OROP (2014/2019) and dearness relief, decisions taken under both governments (OROP accepted in the 2014 President's address and provided ₹1,000 cr in Budget 2014-15 by the incoming government; the 7th CPC was constituted under UPA-II in 2014 and implemented under NDA in 2016). On the contract with soldiers, the Congress objects to Agnipath and promises OROP 'in letter and spirit'; the BJP government defends Agnipath and has paid OROP on the 2015 definition the Supreme Court upheld; identical fields were used for the objection, the Army's answer and the petitioners' contention beside the Court's holding. On states, only Uttar Pradesh (BJP-run) yielded a primary salary share (53.7% of MH 2055 actual 2024-25) and only Bihar (NDA-run) a primary constable pay level (Level 3 ₹21,700); opposition-run West Bengal and Tamil Nadu portals never opened, so no cross-party comparison of pay is made and the asymmetry is recorded as a void rather than a finding. On CAPF outcomes the lens produced rates (14.1 suicides per lakh in 2024; 1.18% VR/resignation) that are workload indicators with two official readings (the Ministry's domestic/health/financial factors; the Committee's working conditions), neither adopted." },
  { domain: "procurement-industry", text: "Vendors: the same fields were computed for every private vendor and for the DPSU in the same category. PIB-named MoD award value 2021-25 (sample of PIB-named releases; joint contracts counted once): DPSUs 77.2% (HAL 58.1%), private Indian vendors 8.7% (L&T ₹24,984.65 crore sole-vendor, 6.1%; Bharat Forge/TASL ₹6,900 crore joint, 1.7%; Bharat Forge/PLR (Adani Defence 51%) ₹2,770 crore joint, 0.68%), the BrahMos JV 5.5%, foreign vendors 5.4%, other PSU 0.7%, a Pinaka EEL/MIL mixed contract 2.5%. Adani Defence beside L&T and Tata Advanced Systems: Adani-linked vendors have no sole-vendor PIB-named award and one joint one; L&T has five sole-vendor awards over 2022-24 including the K9 guns, CIWS and radar; TASL has the 6x6 vehicle half of a joint award and the C-295 consortium role. The identical lens run on DPSUs and on the private vendors returns the same pattern — a few mega-contracts to the platform designer dominate — which is a finding about the lens (concentration by platform design), not about any vendor. Where a DPSU and a private vendor sit on one contract (Pinaka: EEL beside Munitions India) or one category (radars: L&T beside BEL; vessels: L&T and Chowgule beside Goa Shipyard/GRSE/Cochin/HSL; artillery: L&T, Bharat Forge, TASL beside the ordnance successors), the PIB gives joint totals and no bid counts, so no head-to-head can be scored. Private share of production was 19-21% for five years (2019-24) then 22-24%; the private share of exports fell from 72% to 45% as DPSU exports rose from ₹5,874 crore to ₹21,071 crore. Union: UPA-II (FY2009-10 to 2013-14) versus NDA (FY2014-15 to 2025-26) on production, exports and imports: production ₹43,746 crore in 2013-14 against ₹46,429 crore (2014-15) to ₹1.78 lakh crore (2025-26); exports ₹686 crore (2013-14) to ₹38,424 crore; licences 215 (2014) to 788; SIPRI import share 9.1-9.3% (2015-20) to 8.2-8.3% (2020-25); the UPA-II end-year series for production and licences by year was not collected (gap), so growth rates across the two governments are only partly comparable. Cases: Rafale beside Bofors on identical fields — contract date (2016-09-23 against 1986-03-24), value and conversion (€7.87 billion ≈ ₹59,000 crore implied ₹74.96/€ against US$285 million / about ₹1,500 crore in the Bofors record of the money-people file), vendor and intermediaries named, first allegation (2018 Hollande quote against Swedish Radio 1987), investigating body (none appointed for Rafale; CBI for Bofors), courts' outcomes (SC 2018 and 2019 dismissed; Delhi HC 2004/2005 quashed, SC 2018 and 2026 dismissed), the counter-record (CAG: 2.86% below the audit-aligned price; no Indian court or audit found payment to a public servant in either) — the fields line up and neither case produced a conviction; AgustaWestland beside Tatra is carried in money-people.json and literature.json and was not repeated. Bonds: not collected (void)." },
  { domain: "state-police", text: "Same lens on both sides, FY2024-25 (party as text from the fetched CM lists). BJP-run (UP, GJ, MP, RJ, CT, HR, UT, AS, AR, GA, TR): median police revenue spend per capita 2023-24 ₹1,727, median actual police per lakh 174, median vacancy share 20.7%, median NHRC custodial deaths per lakh actual police 6.3. Opposition-run (KA, KL, TN, WB, TG, PB, HP, JH): ₹1,458, 156.5 per lakh, 15.5% vacancy, 7.55 deaths per lakh police. Restricted to large states (pop > 3 crore) the groups overlap on every measure: per capita BJP-run ₹1081–₹1937 vs opposition-run ₹1012–₹2615; custodial-death rates BJP-run 3.51–14.95 vs opposition-run 1.64–9.16. The highest custodial-death counts in both official series are a BJP-run state (Gujarat) and a coalition state (Maharashtra); the thinnest force and the highest FY2025-26 count are in JD(U)-led Bihar; the zero-return states in NCRB 2024 include BJP-run UP and AITC-run West Bengal. What the lens shows is that the outcome series are dominated by reporting practice and small numbers, not by party; the file prints it either way." },
  { domain: "union-defence", text: "Same lens, both governments, Budget Estimates of the four MoD demands summed. UPA-II (BE 2009-10→2013-14): average shares revenue 48.6% / capital 33.8% / pensions 15.6% / MoD civil 2.1%; nominal CAGR of the MoD total 11.0% (GDP at current prices grew 15.3% a year), real CAGR 2.5% deflating by the GNI implicit deflator (Economic Survey 2025-26 Table 1.1). NDA (BE 2014-15→2026-27): average shares revenue 45.3% / capital 26.6% / pensions 23.3% / civil 4.7%; nominal CAGR 8.8% (GDP 10.0%), real CAGR 4.4% over 2014-15→2025-26 (no deflator yet for 2026-27); the first five NDA years alone: nominal 9.1%, real 5.6%. Pay-to-capital (uniformed Pay & Allowances of the three services ÷ Capital Outlay demand, BE): 2000-01 0.71; 2001-02 0.68; 2004-05 0.46; 2005-06 0.47; 2008-09 0.39; 2009-10 0.71; 2010-11 0.67; 2011-12 0.63; 2013-14 0.67; 2014-15 0.71; 2016-17 1.08; 2017-18 1.10; 2018-19 1.10; 2019-20 1.05; 2020-21 1.03; 2021-22 0.87; 2022-23 0.88; 2023-24 0.85; 2024-25 0.85; 2025-26 0.81; 2026-27 0.68. Reading: under both governments the budget grew a little faster than nominal GDP in some years and slower in others, and the pension share rose under both — the step changes are the 2008-09/2009-10 Sixth Pay Commission arrears and OROP (UPA-II and early NDA), the 7th CPC from 2016-17 (NDA), and the RE 2025-26 spectrum-charges line (NDA). The capital share fell under UPA-II and stayed in the high twenties under NDA; the revenue share is the mirror. Neither record is a scandal: both are the arithmetic of a manpower-heavy force paying pensions out of the same envelope. The 2016-17 budget moved Ordnance Factories and R&D (revenue and capital) into the MoD (Misc) demand for one year, which inflates 'civil' and deflates 'revenue' for BE 2015-16/2016-17 in these averages; the four-demand total is unaffected." },
  { domain: "union-home", text: "Union control, run on the same lines with the same fields: UPA-II (FY2009-10 to FY2013-14) beside NDA (FY2014-15 to FY2024-25), actuals. Seven CAPF lines 12.4% a year against 10.1% (Union total expenditure 11.1% against 10.8%); Police demand share of Union expenditure mean 3.05% against 3.30%; CAPF capital share 2.61% against 1.67%; Delhi Police 11.7% against 10.1%; Intelligence Bureau 9.6% (two years) against 13.6%; ED 14.4% against 21.5% (FY2013-14 to FY2024-25); CBI 3.6% against 7.6%; CAPF vacancy rate 14%, 9%, 6% in January 2012 to 2014 against 15% (2017) falling to 7% (2024). Reading: the lines that grew faster under NDA than under UPA-II are the ED, the CBI and the IB; the forces (CAPFs, Delhi Police) did not, and the ED line was already growing at 16% a year in budget estimates before FY2014-15, so the lens gives a mixed picture, not an alarming one for the forces and a real acceleration on the three agency lines from small bases. Date test applied: no ED, CBI or NIA budget is attached to a Home Minister because those agencies are not in the MHA; FY2014-15 and FY2019-20 lines fall across a change of Home Minister and are attributed to neither. The States control (BJP-run beside opposition-run) is not run in this file: the State-wise ASUMP rows carry no ruling-party field and the party-by-year table is not sourced here, so the one State-level fact (zero-release counts by State) is left unsplit for the states agent. Vendor and case controls (Bofors beside Rafale, AgustaWestland beside Tatra) do not apply to this domain." },
];

