        "denialFound": "None found, and none was asked for. The claim is a neutral benchmark that alleges nothing, so no party has anything to respond to.",
        "innocentReading": "India spends close to the peer-group norm. The 2.3% includes paramilitary spending, so it overstates the MoD-only burden, which PRS puts at about 1.9% for 2025-26. The Standing Committee's 3% figure is a domestic target, not a peer norm. These benign readings are already the claim's own reading. Nothing in it implies under- or over-spending or wrongdoing.",
        "reason": "The SIPRI fact sheet, which I opened, confirms every number. Table 1 lists 'India 86.1 ... 2.3', rank 5. The text says 'India, the fifth biggest military spender in the world in 2024, increased its spending to $86.1 billion'. The world burden is 2.5%. Recomputing the median of the 15 burden figures gives 2.3. The claim does not chain facts into an implication. It is a pure comparison, so analytic is the right tier. The weak point is the second source: PRS does not support the 2024 figures. It uses 2023 SIPRI data (India 4th, 2.4%) and a different measure (MoD budget, 1.9% of GDP). It supports only the context about the Standing Committee's 3% figure.",
        "corrections": [
          "(1) Label the PRS source as context only: '2023 SIPRI comparison and Standing Committee 3% recommendation; PRS gives 1.9% MoD budget/GDP for 2025-26, a different measure'. Alternatively, drop it from the srcs of the 2.3%/fifth claim. (2) Add to d: 'SIPRI's India figure includes paramilitary forces; MoD-budget-only burden is lower (PRS: 1.9% in 2025-26).' (3) Add to d or lab: 'tied with the UK at 2.3%'. (4) Optionally re-point s from min:ministry-of-defence to a national 'India (SIPRI)' node, or note that the SIPRI total goes beyond the MoD. (5) The citation 'table 1 and p. 5' is fine. The 'fifth biggest' sentence is in the India paragraph on p. 4-5."
        ],
