        "innocentReading": "The Union's police demand pays for forces the Union itself runs: CAPFs, Delhi Police, J&K Police after 2019, and border infrastructure. State police are paid for from state budgets and are not in this number. The 85% concentration is therefore structural and does not reflect a policy choice. The record already carries this reading.",
        "reason": "I could not refute the claim. Every number matches the source PDF text. Quotes: \"In 2025-26, Rs 1,60,391 crore has been budgeted for police expenditure. A majority of this expenditure (85% of total allocation) is towards the Central Armed Police Forces (CAPF), the Delhi and J&K police, and border infrastructure.\" Table 1 is headed \"69% of the Ministry's expenditure in 2025-26 will be towards police\". \"Rs 21,258 crore has been allocated to Delhi police and J&K police together ... 13% of the total allocation towards police.\" The arithmetic checks out against the BE column of Table 2: CAPF 1,09,037 + Delhi Police 11,932 + J&K Police 9,326 + Border Infrastructure 5,597 = 1,35,892, which is 84.7% of 1,60,391. 11,932 + 9,326 = 21,258. 1,60,391 / 2,33,211 = 68.8%. The claim does not combine separate facts to suggest something none of them says. The tier is 'reported' because it rests on a single credible secondary analysis built from published Budget documents. 'Documented' would be justified once the figure is reproduced from the Union Budget Expenditure Profile or from the MHA demand itself.",
