        "denialFound": "Tyagi has repeatedly denied the allegations. He says the decision to change the specifications was taken by a group of senior officials before his tenure, and his counsel argued in court that \"CBI in the last four years has never been able to establish any evidence against ex-air chief regarding receipt of any bribe\" (https://www.india.com/news/india/sp-tyagi-granted-bail-in-agustawestland-chopper-scam-case-1724022/ ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shashindra_Pal_Tyagi). The UPA government initially said it had \"nothing to hide\" (Wikipedia, citing the JPC debate). Michel contests his detention and the added charges (Bar and Bench, 2026). The edge carries none of these as contra; it carries only the Italian court record as counter-record.",
        "innocentReading": "An FIR and arrests are steps in an investigation, not findings of guilt. Twelve years with no Indian verdict fits ordinary CBI and trial-court delay, plus extradition-specialty litigation, as well as it fits a weak case. The Italian acquittals were for insufficient evidence. They do not prove the absence of a bribe, and they do not prove a bribe either. The claim as worded does not overreach toward guilt.",
        "reason": "I opened the single cited source (the raw text of the Wikipedia article). It states every element of the claim. The FIR line reads: \"registered an FIR on 13 March 2013. The FIR named 13 persons ... The FIR also named four companies\". It reports the CBI arrests of 9 December 2016, Michel's extradition in December 2018, the Milan acquittal of 8 January 2018 \"upheld by the Supreme Court of Cassazione on 22 May 2019\", and Michel remaining \"an accused in the separate CBI corruption case\" in December 2025. 2026 reporting (Delhi HC 2026-04-08, SC hearings May–July 2026) confirms there is still no Indian verdict. Neither lens refutes the claim. One sentence is incomplete: \"Italian courts ended in acquittal\" leaves out that in August 2014 AgustaWestland SpA and AgustaWestland Ltd took a plea bargain (patteggiamento) in Busto Arsizio. That deal involved confiscation of EUR 7.5 million as proceeds of the alleged international corruption, plus administrative fines of EUR 80,000 and EUR 300,000. A patteggiamento is not a finding of guilt, but omitting it makes the claim read more exculpatory than the Italian record is. That calls for a correction, not a kill. The claim rests on one tertiary source whose underlying news reports (The Hindu, TOI) were not opened, so it stays \"reported\". That fits the contract, but it is not \"documented\".",
