        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "In court, SG Tushar Mehta, appearing for the Union, answered the CJI's remark that the government had not cooperated with \"I am not aware of that\" (LiveLaw live-tweets, quoted by The Wire, 2022-08-25, https://thewire.in/law/supreme-court-pegasus-technical-committee). The Union's earlier position was the IT Minister's statement of 2021-07-19 that the reports had \"no factual basis\", plus its refusal on national-security grounds to file a detailed affidavit. The record already carries the second point but not the SG's in-court reply.",
        "innocentReading": "Finding malware on 5 of 29 voluntarily submitted phones fits ordinary commercial malware or poor device security, and the committee itself said it could not attribute the malware to Pegasus. The Union's non-cooperation fits the national-security stance it had already taken before the Court. That is not proof that it procured or used the spyware. The claim, read as written, does not assert either point, so this reading does not contradict it.",
        "reason": "The substance holds. The Wire (2022-08-25) says: \"had found 'malware' in five out of the 29 devices submitted to it. The bench also noted that the Union government 'did not cooperate' with the expert committee... the committee found no conclusive proof of whether Pegasus was the malware used\". Scroll says the same: \"some malware was found on five of the phones... The committee had examined 29 mobile phones... took note of a finding by the panel that the Centre did not co-operate\". Wikipedia also states it, citing Indian Express, Scroll and The Wire. The problem is the source chain in the record. Of its two sources, the indiankanoon link (doc 39021018) is the 2021-10-27 order that set up the committee. It contains nothing about 29 devices, malware on 5, inconclusive findings or non-cooperation, so it cannot support the claim. The other source is a tertiary Wikipedia page. The claim survives on retrieved press reporting, not on the sources the record actually cites. Two independent outlets (The Wire and Scroll, both also cited by Indian Express) would meet the contract's bar for \"documented\". Only upgrade once those outlets are attached. With the record's current sources, \"reported\" is the ceiling.",
