        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "Chavan has called the scandal a \"political accident\" (ThePrint, 2024-04-20, https://theprint.in/india/demolition-regularisation-pleas-pending-chavan-moves-on-but-mumbais-adarsh-society-languishes/2048404/). The HC quashed the sanction because the CBI \"failed to furnish fresh evidence\" (\"In the absence of fresh material, the sanction cannot be sustained\"). The Maharashtra cabinet first rejected the Patil report in Dec 2013 and then partially accepted it. The society's position is that the building is legal, and its appeal against the demolition order is pending in the SC. The record carries no denial or response from Chavan or the society. One should be added.",
        "innocentReading": "Quashing the sanction was a procedural ruling: a second sanction needs fresh material, so the ruling is not a finding of innocence. The SC order keeps the status quo while the appeal is pending and is not a judgment on the merits. \"No verdict\" reflects the pace of the courts, and the case is still pending, not dropped. On symmetry: a UPA-era governor refused sanction (2013), an NDA-era governor granted it (2016), the HC quashed it (2017), and Chavan joined the BJP in Feb 2024. Reading the same facts under both governments gives the same result: an unresolved prosecution under both. Any framing should note the 2024 party switch so the record does not read as targeting one party.",
        "reason": "The claim's own killIf says \"Orders differ in date or scope\", and the SC element triggers it. The claim says \"SC stay 2018\". Primary-adjacent reporting (Deccan Chronicle 22-07-2016; ThePrint 2024) puts the SC order on 22 July 2016 and says it was not a formal stay. It was a direction to the Centre to take possession and not demolish while the appeal is pending, and the Army took possession in July 2016. The rest of the claim checks out against the single Wikipedia source: the 2016-04-29 demolition order, the 2017-12-22 quashing of sanction, and the Patil commission's indictment of 4 ex-CMs, 2 ministers and 12 bureaucrats with 25 illegal allotments and 22 benami. As written, the claim is refuted on the date. With the date corrected and a non-Wikipedia outlet added, it can stay \"reported\". It would reach \"documented\" if the HC orders (2016-04-29, 2017-12-22) and the SC order of 22-07-2016 are opened on indiankanoon or the court portals.",
