        "denialFound": "Not applicable in the usual sense: the claim is itself the exculpatory record for the accused. No statement from Italian prosecutors contesting the 2019 finality was found. Wikipedia says the Milan General Prosecutor's appeal went ahead without the Attorney General's support. The Indian government and CBI kept prosecuting in India after the acquittal. Not asked.",
        "innocentReading": "This is the innocent reading of c071. Italian courts, after a full retrial, found the prosecution's evidence of bribery insufficient, and the highest court made that final. It is a judicial finding of insufficient proof against the two Italian executives, not a finding that no payments to intermediaries occurred and not a ruling on the Indian accused.",
        "reason": "The source says what the claim says. Wikipedia's \"2013 Indian helicopter bribery scandal\" page: \"on 8 January 2018, the Milan Third Court of Appeals finally cleared the defendants and dismissed all charges, on grounds of insufficient evidence ... The verdict was upheld by the Supreme Court of Cassazione on 22 May 2019.\" The underlying reports are independent. Defense News, 22 May 2019 (Tom Kington): \"A former CEO of Italy's Leonardo and a former fellow manager were given a final and definitive acquittal by Italy's highest court on Wednesday in a bribery case dating back to the sale of helicopters to India in 2010.\" Reuters (9 Jan 2018) and The Wire/Scroll/Quint also report the 2018 acquittal. Dates, actors and outcome all match. The cited S. P. Tyagi Wikipedia URL is a redirect to \"Shashindra Pal Tyagi\". That page confirms the Jan 2018 acquittal of Orsi and Spagnolini but says nothing about Cassation in 2019. The claim survives. The tier is right for the sources as cited, which are Wikipedia only. With Reuters 2018 and Defense News 2019 cited directly as two independent credible outlets, it qualifies for documented.",
