        "denialFound": "Not applicable in the usual sense, because the claim is a court action and not an allegation against a party. The Society's position appears in c079: delay, Occupation Certificate, and its intention to appeal. The SC order is effectively the result of that appeal. No statement from the Society on the 22 Jul order was found. Not asked by us.",
        "innocentReading": "This is an ordinary interim order pending the appeal. The SC kept the subject matter intact (no demolition, Centre in possession through the military estate officer, supervised by the HC registrar) until it could hear the appeal. It is not a final ruling on the merits. It neither vindicates nor condemns the Society.",
        "reason": "The Deccan Chronicle source says what the claim says. Quote: \"The top court asked the Centre to take over possession and to ensure that no demolition takes place while securing the controversial Adarsh society building... 'We will secure the building and there will be no demolition,' said Solicitor General Ranjit Kumar to the court. The next hearing of the case has been fixed for August 5.\" Business Standard of 23 Jul 2016 independently reports the same thing (\"Take possession of Adarsh apartments: SC to Centre\", https://www.pressreader.com/india/business-standard/20160723/281522225443621). The claim survives. Tier stays 'reported' rather than 'documented' for two reasons: the SC order itself was not opened, and DC is \"with agency inputs\", so the two outlets may both draw on PTI and may not be independent. There is one problem with how it is framed. DC's URL slug (\"sc-quashes-hc-order\") and its lead sentence (\"ruled against the Bombay High Court order\") overstate what happened. This was an interim order, not a quashing, and the record should not inherit that wording.",
