        "sourceCheck": "partially",
        "denialFound": "Not applicable in the usual sense: this is an analytic comparison with no accused party, and no one was asked. Contrary reading inside the sources: SIPRI's 2026 fact sheet qualifies its own finding. It says the decrease is 'partly' due to domestic capability, 'although there are often substantial delays in domestic production', and that India's planned orders 'including up to 140 combat aircraft from France and 6 submarines from Germany—indicate its continued and probably increasing reliance on foreign suppliers' (https://www.sipri.org/sites/default/files/2026-03/fs_2603_at_2025.pdf). The claim text leaves this out.",
        "innocentReading": "The record's own innocent reading holds. SIPRI TIV measures deliveries of major platforms, not rupee value, so large recent orders (Rafale-M, 114 MRFA, submarines) have not yet been delivered and will show up later. 'Domestic' contracts and 'production' can include imported engines and subsystems (F404, AL-31FP under licence). There are three more boring explanations. First, the whole Asia-Oceania region imported about 20% less, so India's smaller decline fits a regional pattern and needs no policy cause. Second, the production series is nominal: CPI roughly 1.7-1.9x over FY14-FY26 means real growth is about 2-2.3x, not 'almost four times'. Third, the 81% domestic share comes from MoD's own classification of who the vendor is, not from measured indigenous content.",
