        "denialFound": "None found; not asked. This is an analytic claim that alleges no wrongdoing, so no denial is due. For the capital step, ThePrint (https://theprint.in/defence/10x-budget-beef-up-for-intelligence-bureau-capex-after-pahalgam-red-fort-terror-attacks/2842592/) and The Wire (https://m.thewire.in/article/government/74-increase-in-budget-allocations-for-intelligence-bureau-paramilitary-forces-too-get-more-share) both report the ₹2,549.54 cr figure. Neither carries an MHA or official explanation. ThePrint links it to the Pahalgam (April 2025) and Red Fort (November 2025) attacks in its own framing. The Wire says only that the rise 'would involve spending on infrastructure'. No Parliament answer or Detailed Demand was found.",
        "innocentReading": "The IB's mandate grew after 2014: counter-terror coordination, cyber work and the Multi Agency Centre. Pay commission awards (7th CPC in FY2016-17) inflate establishment-heavy lines, though the CAPFs got the same awards and grew more slowly. The two-year UPA-II window is too short for a stable CAGR. Higher inflation in FY12 to FY14 squeezed nominal-to-real growth for every line. The 2026-27 capital provision is a one-off, lumpy technology or infrastructure provision, publicly linked by the press to the 2025 Pahalgam and Red Fort attacks. A BE is also not an actual, and IB capital BEs have historically been under-spent (for example, BE 2025-26 capital 230.76 against RE 257.01; BE 2012-13 capital 44.50 against RE 30.85).",
