        "denialFound": "No denial is needed because the claim alleges no wrongdoing. The MHA's own framing is in the same PIB release (24 JUL 2024, https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2036391): \"number of vacancies as on 01.07.2024 in the Central Armed Police Forces (CAPFs) and Assam Rifles(AR) are 84,106 against total sanctioned strength of 10,45,751. Filling up of vacancies is a continuous process...\" PRS also records the government's remedy: a 10% reservation for ex-Agniveers, plus age and physical-standard relaxations.",
        "innocentReading": "The existing innocentReading fits. CAPFs are a deployable central reserve, not population-proportional policing, so the per-lakh figure measures scale, not coverage. The 15% for 2017 is largely a denominator effect: sanctioned strength jumped from 9.7 to 10.8 lakh in a year while actual strength rose only from 9.0 to 9.2 lakh, so newly sanctioned posts not yet recruited show up as vacancies. Recruitment runs in UPSC/SSC cycles.",
        "reason": "Every number checks against the sources I opened. PRS 2026-27 Table 4: the six force rows sum to sanctioned 1,026,879 and actual 952,764, so vacancy is 7.217%. Dividing both by 14,007.4 lakh gives 73.31 and 68.02 per lakh. PRS states 155 per lakh for January 2024. PRS 2018-19 Table 3 gives 14% (2012), 6% (2014) and 15% (2017). PIB gives 84,106 against 10,45,751. MoSPI's projections reproduce the denominator. The claim is our own derived comparison with no wrongdoing implied and an innocentReading present, so analytic is the correct tier. Nothing refutes it. The remaining defects are presentational: the 2012-17 seven-force series is spliced with the 2024 six-force figure; sanctioned CAPF strength is set beside actual State police strength; and the short text drops the 8.5-11.3% values for 2019-2023, so the arc \"15% to 7.2%\" reads as steadier improvement than the series shows.",
