        "denialFound": "The MHA's own answer gives the reason (LS USQ 4862, https://www.mha.gov.in/MHA1/Par2017/pdfs/par2025-pdfs/LS01042025/4862.pdf): \"Where release is less than allocation, the same is on account of non-submission of Utilization Certificates (UCs)\". On West Bengal it adds: \"had high unspent balances (central share) of around of Rs. 69 cr. as on 01.04.2022, Rs. 39 cr. as on 03.04.2023 and Rs. 11 cr. as on 05.04.2024. Accordingly, the State was not eligible for release\". The BJP's Amit Malviya (https://x.com/amitmalviya/status/1953071100142989412) blames the WB government. I found no response from the West Bengal government on ASUMP releases. It was not asked by this file.",
        "innocentReading": "The Department of Expenditure's release guidelines for centrally sponsored schemes bar a further tranche while unspent balances and utilisation certificates are pending. Most States carry such balances, so releases sit far below allocation everywhere. FY2022-23 was low because ASUMP guidelines were only circulated on 08.08.2022, after the Cabinet extended the scheme on 19.01.2022 (Table II note), and every State got only a token ₹0.33–4.8 cr. From FY2024-25 the SNA-SPARSH fund-flow model added another gate. The pattern is the Union's rules meeting State accounting. It shows no partisan targeting, and the claim asserts none.",
        "reason": "Every State-level figure, ratio and count reproduces exactly from the Parliament answer's Annexure, which I opened and recomputed: 7.1–30.4%, 12 of 28 States, West Bengal nil in 3 of 5 years, 5 States nil in 4. BE 621.45 and RE 152.52 for 2022-23 are confirmed in EB 2023-24, item 28.01. The claim is our own counting with an innocent reading attached and an explicit statement that no ruling-party split was run, so the analytic tier is correct and implies no wrongdoing. Two problems remain, both in the source chain rather than the substance. First, the actual of ₹34.72 cr is not in any cited source. It is printed only in EB 2024-25 (https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/budget2024-25/doc/eb/sbe51.pdf, item 27.01, \"Actual 2022-2023\": 34.72), which the budgets row cites but the claim does not. Second, the cited EB 2026-27 carries 2024-25 to 2026-27 figures and supports none of the claim's numbers.",
