        "denialFound": "None found for the narrative \"Adani is being handed defence\". Adani was not asked. No accusing party is named in the record, and the narrative quotes no Adani or MoD response.",
        "innocentReading": "The carbine order was split between L1 and L2, a normal multi-vendor DAP quantity split after trials that go back to 2005, 2011, 2018 and 2022 (the 2018 Caracal fast-track was cancelled in 2020). Adani has a JV with IWI and an existing small-arms plant at Gwalior. The DcPP role on AEW&C Mk-II is DRDO's standard industry-partner route; it is used for other private and public firms too (e.g. VSHORADS). New entrants with foreign OEM tie-ups (IWI, Elbit) are expected to pick up first-generation orders. None of this shows favour, and it does not support \"only one joint award\" either.",
        "reason": "Two factual parts of the narrative are false as of its own date. (1) \"The only PIB-named award to an Adani-linked vendor is the 2025-12-30 CQB carbine contract\": PIB PRID 2288009 (2026-07-22) names a separate contract signed by CABS/DRDO with ADSTL alone, as DcPP for AEW&C Mk-II mission systems, including integration and flight testing. (2) \"No sole-vendor award to an Adani entity found\": that PIB release is one, and the narrative's own cited source contradicts it too. The AEL Annual Report 2025 page lists deliveries to the Army (ULPGMs, UAVs, the first Drishti-10), to the IAF (micro UAVs, Type II NGARM), surface-to-air missiles to the Navy and IAF, and a counter-drone system to the BSF. Each delivery implies a contract, even though the ₹ is not printed. The 0.68% is arithmetically right but is a share of the file's 45-claim sample, and the text presents it as the whole PIB universe. Overall, \"unsupported\" may still be fair for \"handed defence\", but the counter-case is built on an incomplete search and is overstated. It needs rewriting at analytic tier.",
