        "denialFound": "Yes, but none is recorded in the narrative row. (1) The UPA government initially 'denied all allegations and claimed it had \"nothing to hide\" and that \"our track record is not cover-up.\"' (Wikipedia, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2013_Indian_helicopter_bribery_scandal). (2) Michel 'denied any wrongdoing by stating that he had never met any \"Gandhi\" in his life' (same page). (3) In 2019 the Congress, through Randeep Surjewala, called the ED chargesheet page 'a cheap election stunt'. Ahmed Patel denied wrongdoing and was not named in the CBI FIR (https://www.thequint.com/explainers/explainer-vvip-chopper-scam-agustawestland-congress-bjp-indian-air-force-sp-tyagi; https://www.business-standard.com/article/pti-stories/michel-identified-ap-as-ahmed-patel-ed-chargesheet-in-agustawestland-case-119040401499_1.html). (4) Tyagi has repeatedly denied the allegations.",
        "innocentReading": "Middlemen promise to pay or influence senior figures in order to justify their fees. Michel's note can be read as a sales pitch or a target list, not proof that money arrived; the note itself says these people were to be 'targeted'. Italy's final acquittal (2018, upheld 2019) found insufficient proof of corruption. The service-ceiling change was a joint SPG/NSA/IAF decision with an operational rationale, namely the expansion of the field beyond one vendor. The cancellation was a contract remedy for an integrity-pact breach by the vendor, not a finding that Indian officials took money. Against this, Defence Minister Antony's own statement that 'bribes have been taken' and the encashed guarantees show that some corruption around the deal was accepted by the UPA government itself. The innocent reading covers who received money, not whether any was paid.",
        "reason": "The 'contested' rating holds up. A narrative row cannot be documented: the underlying assertion that bribes reached UPA leaders and the IAF chief comes from CBI and ED chargesheets, Italian prosecutors and a middleman's note, and it is answered by final Italian acquittals and an Indian trial that has not begun. That makes it an allegation carrying a counter, which is what 'contested' means. Nothing in the source contradicts the rating, and the dates and identities line up. The row cannot pass as written, though. It cites one Wikipedia page, which does not contain the service-ceiling claim; that claim is a CBI chargesheet allegation reported by the Deccan Chronicle. Its counter repeats Wikipedia's error that Busto Arsizio 'acquitted the former IAF chief', when Tyagi was not a defendant in Italy. It says the trial is 'pending' when it has not commenced. It leaves out the strongest fact for the claim, Antony's on-record 'bribes have been taken', and it records no denial, although Congress, Patel, Tyagi and Michel all denied. The status stands, but the fields must be corrected.",
